WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.930
ED GLAESER: In the 21st century speak the words,

00:00:02.930 --> 00:00:06.230
city and you conjure images of skyscrapers.

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:10.850
Great urban skylines ARE forests of tall steel framed buildings.

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:14.060
Super tall structures, like the Burj Khalifa in Dubai,

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:16.730
or the Petronas Towers in Kuala Lumpur, are

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:20.750
used to announce the emergence of a city on the global stage.

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:23.990
But for millennia, we had cities without tall towers.

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:26.390
Europe is still dotted with major metropolises,

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:30.040
such as Rome, that are almost completely without skyscrapers.

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:33.650
The skyscraper's invention in the late 19th century

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:38.030
is itself an example of how cities can solve their own problems by allowing

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:40.760
collaborative chains of creativity.

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:43.880
For thousands of years, the Great Pyramid of Giza

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:45.770
was the world's tallest structure standing

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:49.100
as a monument to the coercive powers of the pharaohs,

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:51.740
as well as to Egyptian engineering.

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:54.200
But finally in the 14th century, church spires

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:57.620
began to push above its 481 foot height.

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:02.600
Lincoln Cathedral in England may have reached 525 feet in 1311.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:06.590
But its tall wooden spire was demolished by a violent storm

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:09.020
during the turbulent reign of Edward the Sixth.

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:11.060
After its destruction, the global height record

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:13.430
was held by a series of church towers.

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:16.190
St. Mary's in Strassland, Strasbourg, St. Nikolai

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:19.220
in Hamburg, Notre Dame in Rouen, and finally Cologne,

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:22.550
which was only completed in 1880, and soon lost its preeminence

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:23.960
to the Washington Monument.

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:26.840
And then the towering Eiffel Tower, which

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:31.580
soared 300 meters above Paris, vastly higher than any earlier building.

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:33.920
The Eiffel Tower's wrought iron structure points

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:37.190
to a central engineering element of the skyscraper.

00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:41.360
A steel or cast iron skeleton that bears the weight of the building.

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:44.030
Indeed, a reasonable view is that the skyscraper

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:48.020
can be defined as a tall-ish building, perhaps with at least 10 stories,

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:50.600
with elevators and a metal frame.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:53.540
This definition emphasizes that the skyscraper

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:58.280
depended on combining two 19th century technologies, high quality metal

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:01.010
production, and safe elevators.

00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:03.920
Metal had been used in building for over a millenium.

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:05.900
The Chinese, who were master metalworkers,

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:08.570
produced cast iron pagodas in the seventh century.

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:11.510
But before the Industrial Revolution, metal

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:14.910
was expensive and uneven in quality.

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:17.630
In fact, one reason why James Watt moved from Glasgow

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:19.520
to make his separate condenser steam engines,

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:22.730
was that he needed high quality metal cylinders, which

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:26.720
could be supplied in Birmingham from the nearby furnaces of the legendary John

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:28.460
Iron Mad Wilkinson.

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:31.560
Great innovations regularly require collaboration,

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:33.470
which is why cities like Watt's Birmingham,

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:36.050
have so often been hubs of invention.

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:39.710
The Industrial Revolution powered by Watt's steam engines,

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:43.370
turned high quality iron into a plausible building material.

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:47.420
Wilkinson himself played a major role in building the world's first cast iron

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:48.680
bridge.

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:51.800
William Strutt built textile mills with wooden beams

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:55.760
that were encased by sheet iron to reduce the risk of fires.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:58.730
Charles Bage did him one better by building the Ditherington Flax

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:02.030
Mill with an entirely iron frame.

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:05.930
This may be the world's first iron frame building and a proto skyscraper.

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:09.920
An iron frame means that the weight of the building is borne not by the walls,

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:11.570
but by a metal skeleton.

00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:15.890
The facade is hung like a sheet on this underlying frame.

00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:18.110
So while traditional mass wall construction

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:21.650
requires unbelievably thick walls, or perhaps flying

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:24.500
buttresses to support great height, framed buildings

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:27.680
can have thin light walls, even walls of glass,

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:30.530
since the skeleton is actually holding up the structure.

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:34.010
Consequently metal frame buildings became particularly popular

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:37.100
for uses that benefit from light.

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:39.680
A.T. Stewart was one of New York's greatest merchants,

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:42.680
and his salesmanship made him one of the richest men in history.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:46.520
He realized that displaying merchandise on the ground floors of his stores

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:49.730
would lure in New York City's heavy pedestrian traffic.

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:54.620
And so he built his 1848 marble palace with big windows facing the street.

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:56.751
The windows made the ground floor was weak,

00:03:56.751 --> 00:03:59.000
and to hold up the rest of the structure his architect

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:01.520
used cast iron columns for support.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:06.170
In 1862, Stewart brought in even more light with his iron palace built

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:08.600
with a complete cast iron skeleton.

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:11.120
Windows are even more useful in greenhouses,

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:15.290
which is why Joseph Paxton, a towering figure in Victorian horticulture,

00:04:15.290 --> 00:04:18.019
became an artist when he built with cast iron.

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:20.029
Paxton won the competition to build a home

00:04:20.029 --> 00:04:23.060
for London's Great Exhibition of 1851.

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:26.840
His crystal palace was another precursor to the skyscraper,

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:32.300
and it encompassed almost one million square feet, with 4,000 tons of iron,

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:34.880
and 900,000 square feet of glass.

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:38.540
Paxton would influence other architects, like Victor Baltard, who

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:41.900
used cast iron to build Paris' central market, Les Halles,

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:44.840
and the iron beam supported church of St. Augustine.

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:48.110
New York built its own crystal palace in 1853.

00:04:48.110 --> 00:04:52.400
And the next year, Elijah Otis dazzled the crowds within its walls.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:56.210
He stood high on an elevator and ordered the rope holding it to be cut.

00:04:56.210 --> 00:04:59.940
His fall was almost immediately broken by the safety catch

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:01.410
that Otis had designed.

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:04.420
Otis' elevators made it safe to move vertically.

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:08.600
And in 1870, New York's iron framed Equitable Life Assurance building

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:13.580
rose to 130 feet, and became the first office building to offer elevators.

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:16.130
High floors had been tough to rent because tenants

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:18.380
disliked walking up all those stairs.

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:21.470
Otis changed all that, and unleashed the commercial possibilities

00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:23.360
of building up.

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:25.340
Architectural historians have often referred

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:28.760
to Chicago's Home Insurance Building, built in 1884,

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:30.890
as the world's first skyscraper.

00:05:30.890 --> 00:05:33.260
And its architect William Le Baron Jenney,

00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:36.230
has often been called the father of the skyscraper.

00:05:36.230 --> 00:05:38.960
But naturally, there's a lively architectural history

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:43.970
debate about this Home Insurance only has steel framing on the outer walls.

00:05:43.970 --> 00:05:46.610
Moreover, home insurance is only eight feet taller

00:05:46.610 --> 00:05:48.590
than New York's earlier Equitable Building,

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:51.189
although it does have 10 rather than seven stories.

00:05:51.189 --> 00:05:54.230
But the Montauk Building, erected a few years earlier than Home Insurance

00:05:54.230 --> 00:05:57.160
by Daniel Burnham and John Root, also had 10 stories.

00:05:57.160 --> 00:06:00.680
Burnham and Root would build Chicago's towering 21

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:02.747
story Masonic temple a few years later.

00:06:02.747 --> 00:06:05.330
Burnham would also bring the innovations of the Chicago school

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:07.690
into New York with his Flat Iron building.

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:11.360
Maybe Burnham or Root are the real fathers of the skyscraper.

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:14.210
Or maybe Louis Sullivan, whose architectural vision

00:06:14.210 --> 00:06:19.100
imagined skyscrapers that fully embraced the potential of the steel frame,

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:22.070
or maybe Peter B. White, the great fireproofing engineer,

00:06:22.070 --> 00:06:23.690
really deserves the credit.

00:06:23.690 --> 00:06:26.600
I think that the search for any single father of the skyscraper

00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:29.810
is actually a fool's errand, for the skyscraper,

00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:35.300
like pretty much every other great human invention, is a collaborative creation.

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:37.850
All of these Chicago architects knew each other.

00:06:37.850 --> 00:06:40.760
Sullivan and Burnham both worked as apprentices for Jenney.

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:43.310
They all liberally borrowed ideas from one another.

00:06:43.310 --> 00:06:45.410
Jenney made a first step that relied heavily

00:06:45.410 --> 00:06:48.050
on even earlier innovations from Birmingham to New York.

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:50.360
And the others riffed on his start.

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:53.754
If anything, the city of Chicago deserves the credit,

00:06:53.754 --> 00:06:56.420
for it assembled a remarkable collection of architectural talent

00:06:56.420 --> 00:06:58.790
to rebuild after its great fire.

00:06:58.790 --> 00:07:03.290
That talent, connected by urban density collaboratively

00:07:03.290 --> 00:07:08.210
figured out how to make buildings that soared, and that soared beautifully.

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:13.070
The skyscraper is a reminder of how cities solve their own problems.

00:07:13.070 --> 00:07:17.990
In this case the need to fit more usable space on a finite amount of land

00:07:17.990 --> 00:07:21.760
by enabling smart people to learn from one another.

